<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s07e07" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 7: Dividing with Variables</title>
<assemblage>
  <title>Episode 7 Learning Goals</title>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Interpret and perform calculations involving negative exponents
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Interpret and perform calculations involving zero exponent
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Divide a polynomial by a term.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</assemblage>
<p>
  Let's think about what happens in this situation:
  <md>
    \frac{x^5}{x^2}
  </md>
  Let’s unpack the exponents:
  <md>
    \frac{x\cdot x\cdot x\cdot x\cdot x}{x \cdot x}
  </md>
  Each divided x cancels out one of the multiplied x’s:
  <md>
    x \cdot x \cdot x = x^3
  </md>
  Notice that we ended up subtracting the exponents:
  <md>\frac{x^5}{x^2 = x^{5-2}</md>
</p>
<assemblage>
  <title>Exponent Property Four</title>
  <p>
    Let <m>m</m> and<m>n</m> stand for two numbers (that may be different from problem to problem). 
  </p>
  <p>
    If you have the base <m>x</m> raised to a power, and then you divide by that same based raised to a different power, you should subtract the two exponents.
    <md>\frac{x^m}{x^n}=x^{m-n}</md>
  </p>
</assemblage>
<example>
  <p>
    Simplify
    <md>
      \frac{x^{70}}{x^{42}}
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    Solution: It would be tedious to write out 70 and 42 x’s, so we use an exponent property.
    <md>
      \frac{x^{70}}{x^{42}}= x^{70-42}=x^{28}
    </md>
  </p>
</example>
<assemblage>
  <title>Exponent Property Five </title>
  <p>
    If you see something (besides zero) raised to the power 0, the result of that is 1:
    <md>
      x^0=1
    </md>
  </p>
</assemblage>
<assemblage>
  <title>Makes Sense, Right?</title>
  <p>
    If we simplified
    <md>
      \frac{x^3}{x^3}
    </md>
    using the exponent property, we would get
    <md>
      \frac{x^3}{x^3} = x^{3-3} = x^0
    </md>
    But at the same time, when you divide something by itself, the result is always 1:
    <md>
      \frac{x^3}{x^3} = 1
    </md>
    That’s why it makes sense to have <m>x^{0}=1</m>.
  </p>
</assemblage>
<assemblage>
  <title>Exponent Property Six</title>
  <p>
    If you see something raised to a negative power, that’s asking you to take the reciprocal:
    <md>
      x^{-4} = \frac{1}{x^4}
    </md>
  </p>
</assemblage>
<assemblage>
  <title>Makes Sense, Right?</title>
  <p>
    If we calculated
    <md>
      \frac{x^3}{x^5}
    </md>
   using the exponent property, we would get
   <md>
    \frac{x^3}{x^5} = x^{3-5}=x^{-2}
   </md>
   But at the same time, if we unpack the exponents:
   <md>
    \frac{x^3}{x^5} = \frac{x\cdot x\cdot x}{x\cdot x \cdot x\cdot x \cdot x}
   </md>
   Three of the multiplied x’s are cancelled by three of the divided x’s, leaving:
   <md>
    \frac{1}{x\cdot x} = \frac{1}{x^2}
   </md>
   That’s why it makes sense to have <m>x^{-2}=\frac{1}{x^2}</m>
  </p>
</assemblage>
<p>
  When we are dividing a polynomial by a term
  <md>
    \frac{2x+6}{3}
  </md>
  you can divide each part of the polynomial separately:
  <md>
    \frac{2x}{3}+\frac{6}{3}
  </md>
<md>
\frac{2x}{3}+2
</md>
</p>
<assemblage>
  <title>Makes Sense, Right?</title>
  <p>
    When we learned about fractions, we learned that when we were adding or subtracting two fractions with the same denominator, we just need to add the numerators:
    <md>
      \frac{2}{7}+\frac{4}{7} = \frac{2+4}{7}
    </md>
    When we divide a polynomial, we’re just following this process backward.
    <md>
      \frac{2+4}{7}=\frac{2}{7}+\frac{4}{7} 
    </md>
  </p>
</assemblage>
<example>
  <p>
    Divide
    <md>
      \frac{9x^5-x^4+x^3}{x^2}
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    Solution: Divide each term of the numerator separately:
    <md>
      \frac{9x^5}{x^2}-\frac{x^4}{x^4}+\frac{x^3}{x^2}
    </md>
    Then we can use the exponent properties to simplify each piece:
    <md>
      9x^{5-2}-x^{4-2}+x^{3-2}
    </md>
    <md>
      9x^3-x^2+x
    </md>
  </p>
</example>
<example>
  <p>
    Divide
    <md>
      \frac{6x^2+8x}{2x}
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    Solution: Divide each term of the numerator separately:
    <md>
      \frac{6x^2}{2x} + \frac{8x}{2x}
    </md>
    In the first fraction, the <m>6\div 2</m> becomes 3, and the <m>x^{2}\div x</m> becomes <m>x</m>.
    In the second fraction, the <m>8\div 2</m> becomes 4, and the <m>x\div x</m> cancels out to 1.
    <md>
      3x+4
    </md>
  </p>
</example>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Exponent Properties</title>
    <p>
      Here is a list of the exponent properties from Episodes 6 and 7
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>x^m x^n = x^{m+n}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>(x^a)^b = x^{a\cdot b}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>(xy)^p = x^p y^p</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
              <m>\frac{x^m}{x^n}=x^{m-n}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>x^0=1</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>x^{-p} = \frac{1}{x^p}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s07e07">
    <title>Season 7, Episode 7 Practice Problems</title>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
      <p>
        Use the exponent properties to simplify
      </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>x^{120}/x^{35}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>z^{56}/z^{19}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>b^{250}/b^{150}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y^{200}/y^{78}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>a^{399}/a^{12}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>m^{87}/m^{42}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
      <p>
        Use the exponent properties to simplify
      </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>x^{-5}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y^{-12}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>a^{0}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>7^{0}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>3^{-4}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>9^{-2}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
